# Nova v4 secrets — deploy/install.sh copies this to deploy/.env on first
# run and fills in the blanks with `openssl rand -hex 32`. Re-running the
# installer preserves whatever values are already set here.
#
# ── `# nova-backup:` ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every key below carries a disposition on the comment line IMMEDIATELY above
# it — one line, nothing between it and the key — and a key in a real
# deploy/.env that has no declaration here REFUSES the backup by name
# (R2_UNCLASSIFIED) rather than being quietly carried or quietly dropped.
# Three values:
#
#   carry  travels in the bundle (env/carried.env) and is written into the
#          target's .env on restore. Everything that is the same Nova
#          wherever it runs: the secrets the databases and the services were
#          created with.
#   host   belongs to the machine, never travels. Absolute paths, this
#          network's addresses, a key that is spent on first use. Carrying
#          one would make the restore fight the installer that just ran.
#   drop   declared so the backup does not refuse, and carried nowhere. Dead
#          config that a real .env may still hold.
#
# Two keys are declared here as COMMENTS because install.sh writes them into a
# real .env and this file never holds them: without a declaration, every
# existing install would refuse on its first backup.

# nova-backup: carry
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
# nova-backup: carry
CORE_TOKEN=
# nova-backup: carry
CORE_GATEWAY_TOKEN=
# nova-backup: carry
CORE_MEMORY_TOKEN=
# Signs the bundled SearXNG's own result URLs. Left blank here so install.sh
# generates one; docker-compose.yml falls back to a shared dev default if it is
# still blank, so search works even without running the installer.
# nova-backup: carry
SEARXNG_SECRET=

# OPT-IN, and install.sh deliberately never generates this one. Blank = the
# web origin's pre-auth gate is off (the default: normal Tailscale/localhost
# access). Set it yourself (e.g. `openssl rand -hex 32`) only when you are
# temporarily putting :3000 on a public URL — a cloudflared quick tunnel,
# say — and want a token required before anyone sees anything Nova serves.
# See apps/web/nginx.conf.template.
# The operator chose this value; a restore keeps it.
# nova-backup: carry
NOVA_PUBLIC_GATE_TOKEN=

# Fixed addresses on the project network (docker-compose.yml pins the
# network's subnet so these CAN be fixed). NOVA_WEB_ADDR is where the tailnet
# sidecar's `tailscale serve` finds nginx; NOVA_TAILSCALE_ADDR is the
# sidecar's own address — the ONE source address nginx trusts a
# Tailscale-User-* identity header from, which is what exempts a tailnet
# peer from the gate above. Both sit in the upper half of the subnet, outside
# dynamic allocation. Blank takes the defaults shown; change them only
# together with the subnet in docker-compose.yml.
# Leave blank for the defaults (172.18.128.10 and 172.18.128.20). A comment
# on a value line becomes PART of the value — compose does not strip it.
# Both are addresses on THIS machine's docker network.
# nova-backup: host
NOVA_WEB_ADDR=
# nova-backup: host
NOVA_TAILSCALE_ADDR=

# ---- Tailnet access (the `tailnet` profile; deploy/README.md) -------------
# Opt-in: `NOVA_TAILNET=1 ./install` turns the profile on and fills these in.
#
# A Tailscale auth key from https://login.tailscale.com/admin/settings/keys —
# the ONE input only you hold. It is used once, on the node's first login;
# after that the identity lives on the v4_tailscale volume and the key is no
# longer consulted, so a NON-reusable key (the default kind) is the right
# choice: it lingers in this file but cannot join a second node. install.sh
# never generates one and refuses the profile without it — unless the volume
# already holds a logged-in node (a re-install, or the migrated old node).
# Blank it out after the first `up` if you like; nothing breaks.
# It is spent on the first login and the node identity then lives on
# v4_tailscale, which travels under --move only — so this key never travels.
# nova-backup: host
TS_AUTHKEY=
# The node's name on your tailnet: the URL becomes
# https://<TAILNET_HOSTNAME>.<your-tailnet>.ts.net. Blank = nova.
# The URL people have saved is part of this Nova, so it travels.
# nova-backup: carry
TAILNET_HOSTNAME=
# Which optional services a bare `docker compose up -d` includes, comma-
# separated (compose reads this file). install.sh writes every profile it
# started here — `inference` for the bundled ollama, `tailnet` when that
# profile is on — derived from the --profile flags it passed, and takes one
# out only on its explicit off-switch (NOVA_SKIP_INFERENCE=1, NOVA_TAILNET=0).
# A `--profile X` flag on the command line REPLACES this list rather than
# adding to it — so after installing, prefer plain `docker compose up -d`.
# Leave blank for none.
# The target machine's own install decides its profiles: a host with no
# NVIDIA runtime must not be handed `inference`.
# nova-backup: host
COMPOSE_PROFILES=

# COMPOSE_FILE — the compose files a plain `docker compose` merges, ':'
# between them. install.sh ADDS this line itself, with ABSOLUTE paths
# (docker-compose.yml, plus docker-compose.gpu.yml when docker has the NVIDIA
# runtime — without it ollama comes up on the CPU and every healthcheck stays
# green). It is deliberately NOT pre-seeded here: an empty `COMPOSE_FILE=`
# makes compose resolve "" to your working directory and fail with "read /:
# is a directory" (measured 2026-09-04), which would break a bare
# `docker compose up` from a hand-copied copy of this file. Two facts: any -f
# on the command line REPLACES the whole list, and a relative entry resolves
# from the shell's working directory, not from this file's — hence absolute.
# Run compose from deploy/, or with `--project-directory deploy`.
#
# Declared, not seeded: the key is absent from this file and present in every
# real .env, so the declaration has to sit on a commented-out key or the
# first backup of an existing install refuses.
# Absolute paths into THIS checkout, plus this machine's GPU-overlay decision.
# nova-backup: host
# COMPOSE_FILE=

# INSTANCE_SECRET — dead config. It used to be generated in install.sh;
# nothing in any service reads it (sessions are DB-hashed random tokens) and
# it was dropped at S2 seam-hygiene. An .env written before that change still
# carries a stale value, and install.sh deliberately leaves the line where it
# is rather than editing a file it had no other reason to touch
# (deploy/install.sh:20-25). Measured on the live stack 2026-09-21
# (s41/measurements.md R7): it is really there.
# Carrying a value nothing reads would put a stale secret on the new machine
# and imply it means something.
# nova-backup: drop
# INSTANCE_SECRET=
